      "heading": "TF-IDF: rare words carry the signal",
      "narration": "Raw counts have an obvious problem, and fixing it gives us the workhorse of classical text retrieval. The most common words in any document are 'the', 'of', 'and', 'is'. They will dominate every vector, and they distinguish absolutely nothing, because they are present in every document ever written. What you actually want to weight heavily is the opposite: words that are rare across the corpus but frequent in this particular document. That is TF-IDF, term frequency times inverse document frequency. The formula is: the count of the word in this document, multiplied by the log of N over df, where N is the total number of documents and df is how many of them contain the word at all. Look at what that log term does. A word appearing in every document has df equal to N, so N over df is one, and log of one is zero — the word is annihilated. It contributes nothing, automatically, with no stopword list needed. A word appearing in three documents out of a million gets log of about three hundred thousand, a large multiplier. So 'Poneglyph' outweighs 'the' by an enormous factor, exactly as it should. Now here is my favourite part of this lesson, because it connects two islands you already visited. That inverse document frequency term is essentially the self-information from the information theory lesson. Log of one over probability. A rare word is a surprising word, and a surprising word is an informative word. The very same quantity that defined entropy is now ranking your search results. This is not a coincidence or an analogy — Shannon's measure of surprise turns out to be the correct measure of what a word tells you about a document. One final step that people skip and then wonder why their search is broken: L2-normalize every document vector, dividing it by its own length. Without this, a five-thousand-word chapter has a vector far longer than a two-hundred-word note, and the long one wins every similarity comparison purely for being long. After normalization, every document sits on the unit sphere and only its direction — its mix of topics — matters.",
