      "heading": "Cosine search: a working search engine in one dot product",
      "narration": "Now we cash all of this in, and the payoff is that you can build a real search engine this afternoon. Take the user's query — 'ancient weapon location' — and treat it as a very short document. Vectorize it with exactly the same vocabulary and exactly the same idf weights you computed over the corpus; this must be the same transformation, not a fresh one, or the dimensions do not correspond. Now score every document by the cosine similarity between the query vector and the document vector, and sort. That is it. That is a search engine, and the ranking function is the dot product from the very first lesson of this course. Why cosine rather than plain Euclidean distance? Because cosine ignores magnitude entirely and compares only direction. Two documents about navigation point the same way whether one is a paragraph or a book. Euclidean distance would call the long one and the short one dissimilar simply because one vector is longer, which is precisely the wrong invariance for text. And note the small elegance: once you have L2-normalized everything, the cosine and the dot product are literally the same number, and the division disappears from your code. Then there is the trick that makes it fast enough to be real. Your query has maybe five non-zero terms. Any document that shares none of those five words scores exactly zero, and there is no point computing that. So you precompute an inverted index — a map from each word to the list of documents containing it — look up only the five posting lists, and score only the union of those candidates. That is how a query returns in milliseconds across millions of documents, and it is the same structure that has powered search since the seventies. Finally, notice that vectorizing the text was the entire job. Those same TF-IDF vectors feed straight into logistic regression from the last lesson, and you have a spam classifier. Feed them into the clustering we do next lesson, and you have automatic topic discovery. The models did not change at all. Once text is vectors, every tool you already own applies to it.",
