      "heading": "Practical: the preprocessing decisions that actually move the score",
      "narration": "Let me close with the decisions that actually change your numbers, because textbooks list ten preprocessing steps and in practice about four of them matter. Lowercasing. It merges 'Ship' at the start of a sentence with 'ship' in the middle, which is almost always what you want, and it halves your vocabulary. But be aware of what it destroys: 'US' the country becomes 'us' the pronoun, 'IT' the department becomes 'it'. For topic classification, lowercase freely. For named-entity extraction, do not. Stemming and lemmatization. Both try to collapse 'running', 'runs' and 'ran' into one feature. Stemming does it by chopping suffixes with crude rules, which is fast and sometimes produces non-words — 'universe' becomes 'univers'. Lemmatization uses an actual dictionary and grammatical analysis, which is slower and correct. My honest advice: try without either first. On a large corpus, the different forms of a word each get enough examples that the model handles them fine, and you may find the whole step changes your score by nothing at all. Now the two dials that give the biggest return for the least thought: minimum and maximum document frequency. Drop every word that appears in fewer than about three documents — those are overwhelmingly typos, scanning errors and one-off noise, and they are pure variance. Drop every word appearing in more than about ninety percent of documents — those are stopwords by measurement rather than by list, and they carry no signal. These two settings routinely cut the vocabulary in half with no loss of accuracy, and a smaller vocabulary means faster training and less overfitting. And finally, the rule that outranks all of the above. Fit the vectorizer on the training set only. Building the vocabulary and computing the idf weights over your whole dataset before you split it means the training features were chosen with knowledge of the test documents. Your reported score goes up, your real performance does not, and nothing anywhere raises an error. This is the single most common bug in applied text machine learning, and it is invisible unless you look for it deliberately.",
